{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-11193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}}{{10}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-11193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}}{{10}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\cdot {(-3)}^{n}}{{10}^{n}} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{{(-3)}^{n}}{{10}^{n}} \\\\  & =-3{(-\\frac{3}{10})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=-\\frac{3}{10}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-11193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "024681eacd50d272bc000914d0dae0ae76e6f051f0fce1d4415fcab82dd2bdfd"} {"question" "q-11193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "ca