fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx\\ge \\sqrt{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-141332" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "024681eacd50d272bc000914d0dae0ae76e6f051f0fce1d4415fcab82dd2bdfd"} {"question" "q-141332" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-141332" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0<x\\le 1"] ["math" "0<x\\le \\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "x\\ge 1"] ["math" "x\\ge 2"] ["math" "x\\ge \\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-141332" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\ge \\sqrt{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-141318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the geometric sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{x}{(2x)}^{n},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-141318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=2x.$ \nSince $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<2x\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-141318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "024681eacd50d272bc000914d0dae0ae76e6f051f0fce1d4415fcab82dd2bdfd"} {"question" "q-141318" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-141318" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0<x\\le 1"] ["math" "0<x\\le 2"] ["math" "0<x\\le \\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "x\\ge 1"] ["math" "x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-141318" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0<x\\le \\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-74633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{2}^{n+4}}{{x}^{n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-74633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{2}^{n+4}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{n}\\cdot {2}^{4}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & ={2}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =16{(\\frac{2}{x})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{2}{x}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,x\\ge 2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-74633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "024681eacd50d272bc000914d0dae0ae76e6f051f0fce1d4415fcab82dd2bdfd"} {"question" "q-74633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-74633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0<x<2"] ["math" "0<x\\le 4"] ["math" "x>2"] ["math" "x\\ge 2"] ["math" "x\\ge 4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-74633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\ge 2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-141336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{16}^{n+1}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-141336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{16}^{n+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{16\\cdot {16}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{16}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}.$ Since ${x}^{2}$ is positive, $r$ is also positive.  So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0<\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<{x}^{2}\\le 16.\n$$\n\nTaking into account that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-141336" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "024681eacd50d272bc000914d0dae0ae76e6f051f0fce1d4415fcab82dd2bdfd"} {"question" "q-141336" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-141336" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0<x\\le 1"] ["math" "0<x\\le 4"] ["math" "0<x\\le 8"] ["math" "x\\ge 4"] ["math" "x\\ge 8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-141336" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0<x\\le 4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-351624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{4-n}}{{x}^{5n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ Find the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges.\n\n$x$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-351624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{4-n}}{{x}^{5n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{-n}}{{x}^{5n}} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{6n}} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}.$ Since ${x}^{6}$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,{x}^{6}\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx\\ge 1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-351624" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "024681eacd50d272bc000914d0dae0ae76e6f051f0fce1d4415fcab82dd2bdfd"} {"question" "q-351624" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-351624" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\ge"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-351624" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-351624" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-123344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{{4}^{n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-123344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2n}\\cdot x}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & =x\\cdot {(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.$ Since ${x}^{2}$ is positive, $r=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}$ is also positive.  So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0<\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<{x}^{2}\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTaking into account that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-123344" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "024681eacd50d272bc000914d0dae0ae76e6f051f0fce1d4415fcab82dd2bdfd"} {"question" "q-123344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-123344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0<x\\le 1"] ["math" "0<x\\le 2"] ["math" "x>0"] ["math" "x\\ge 1"] ["math" "x\\ge 2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-123344" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0<x\\le 2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-351812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{3n+2}}{{x}^{2n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ Find the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$<x$\n\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-351812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{3n+2}}{{x}^{2n}} \\\\  & ={x}^{\\,3n+2-2n} \\\\  & ={x}^{\\,n+2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=x.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0<x\\le 1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-351812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "024681eacd50d272bc000914d0dae0ae76e6f051f0fce1d4415fcab82dd2bdfd"} {"question" "q-351812" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-351812" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-351812" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-351812" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\le"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-351812" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "q-351812" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e394b0498f7c0cd247c708f1eb97231f17f2a2ac880e99ea31c626105221cc1"} {"question" "e-647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Does the sequence $a_n = \\dfrac {2 \\cdot (-3) ^ n}{5 ^ {n + 1}}$ for $n\\geq 1$ converge or diverge?\nIf it converges, what is its limit?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/example-647.json" "file_sha256" "08b39b07c27906cba1b7e41118fa8bbe9fa3c37cd9c874a99946e5a74b3e1972"} {"question" "e-647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r| <           1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r| > 1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n\n- if $r = -1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form $a_n = a \\cdot r^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-3)^{n}}{{5}^{n+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-3)^{n}}{5\\cdot {5}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{(-3)^{n}}{{5}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=-\\dfrac{3}{5} .$ \n\nSince $|r| < 1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/example-647.json" "file_sha256" "08b39b07c27906cba1b7e41118fa8bbe9fa3c37cd9c874a99946e5a74b3e1972"} {"question" "e-649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the geometric sequence \n\n$$\na_n =\\dfrac{x^2}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{16}{x^2}\\right)^{n}, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is a positive real number. For which values of $x$ is the sequence convergent?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/example-649.json" "file_sha256" "01866c782dfd4217360637dcb8239ec04b6a2b81a8bf3571ca72b6325703f042"} {"question" "e-649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r| <                      1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r| > 1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n\n- if $r = -1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r = \\dfrac{16}{x^2}.$ \nSince $x^2$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is $0 \\lt r\\leq 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0 \\lt\\dfrac{16}{x^2}\\leq 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x^2 \\geq 16.\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx \\geq 4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/example-649.json" "file_sha256" "01866c782dfd4217360637dcb8239ec04b6a2b81a8bf3571ca72b6325703f042"} {"question" "e-9990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the sequence $a_n = \\dfrac{x^{2n+1}}{x^{n-2}},$ where $x$ is a real positive number and $n\\geq 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/example-9990.json" "file_sha256" "e56735f8e9052327d23e2c932aff0fe3b6bef03871ed528767fbdb9b6432ccf4"} {"question" "e-9990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r| <              1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r| > 1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n\n- if $r = -1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form $a_n = a \\cdot r^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{{x}^{n-2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2n+1-n+2} \\\\  & ={x}^{n+3} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with common ratio $r = x.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0 \\lt  r \\leq 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0 \\lt  x \\leq 1 .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031858/example-9990.json" "file_sha256" "e56735f8e9052327d23e2c932aff0fe3b6bef03871ed528767fbdb9b6432ccf4"}] "usage" {"e-9990" [] "e-647" [] "e-649" []} "basis" 1216 "retractions" [[17592186184024 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r$. Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $\\mid r \\mid < 1$, the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n- if $\\mid r \\mid > 1$, the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1$, the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n- if $r = -1$, the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form\n\n$$\na_n = a \\cdot r^{n}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= (2 \\cdot (-3)^{n})/(5^{n + 1}) \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot (-3)^{n})/(5 \\cdot 5^{n}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot ((-3)^{n})/(5^{n}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}(-\\frac{3}{5})^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with\n\n$$\nr=-\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mid r \\mid < 1$, the sequence converges to $0$."] [17592186184025 :question/problem "Consider the geometric sequence\n$a_n =\\dfrac{x^2}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{16}{x^2}\\right)^{n}, \\qquad n\\geq 1$\n\nwhere $x$ is a positive real number. For which values of $x$ is the sequence convergent?"] [17592186184025 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r$. Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $\\mid r \\mid < 1$, the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n- if $\\mid r \\mid > 1$, the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1$, the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n- if $r = -1$, the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{16}{x^2}\n$$\n\nSince $x^2$ is positive, $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is\n\n$$\n0 \\lt r\\leq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0 \\lt\\dfrac{16}{x^2}\\leq 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x^2 \\geq 16\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx \\geq 4\n$$"] [17592186184078 :question/problem "Consider the sequence $a_n = \\dfrac{x^{2n+1}}{x^{n-2}}$, where $x$ is a real positive number and $n\\geq 1$. What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?"] [17592186184078 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r$. Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $\\mid r \\mid < 1$, the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n- if $\\mid r \\mid > 1$, the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1$, the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n- if $r = -1$, the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form\n\n$$\na_n = a \\cdot r^{n}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= \\frac{x^{2n + 1}}{x^{n - 2}} \\\\\n&= x^{2n + 1 - n + 2} \\\\\n&= x^{n + 3} \\\\\n&= x^{3} \\cdot x^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with common ratio $r = x$. Since $x$ is positive, $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is\n\n$$\n0 \\lt r \\leq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0 \\lt x \\leq 1\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
